to take over with a  couple weeks  preparation and he catalogs all of the  problems he has to face  and they're unbelievable i mean it's  astonishing when you think about how  hard this task was  and one of the theses in the book  michael and mick  is i'll read it i was really struck by  it is there is a direct link between the  way the iraq war was planned and the  bitter insurgency the american-led  coalition subsequently confronted and i  think that's right there is a link i  don't deny it but i think there's a  stronger link between the ethnic  geography of iraq and the bitter  insurgency and between the saddam  history in that area and the bitter  insurgency i think if there's any if  there's criticism i'd offer about the  book cause i think it underp it  downplays a little bit  the impact of that history in iraq  and perhaps exaggerate  the  impact of the mistakes we made in the  post-war planning  and occupation effort i don't doubt that  there were mistakes and i don't doubt  that they had a role but their relative  weight as opposed to what was the what  were the facts on the ground when we got  in there i think might have been a  little bit um misplaced last point kurt  there is a sense in this book that um  transformation of the military was  oversold a bit  and and i worry about that i i worry  about taking too much from that clearly  the post-war planning and occupation was  mishandled  but the war itself  uh was a pretty extraordinary event when  you think about it um i mean you take  this you know  we can dismiss them however we want and  i think the information you have about  the iraqi strategy is terrific but this  was the fifth largest army in the world  uh and it was uh defeated effectively in  three weeks  with hardly any air campaign beforehand  with one division less  than even the planners had counted on  because the fourth wasn't available from  turkey  this was a fraction of what the military  conventional wisdom was beforehand it  would have and they did it  and it's pretty extraordinary and it  says a lot  uh i think it validates a lot of the  ideas that were in the president's  citadel speech in 99 and that secretary  rumsfeld carried through  and clearly  those lessons should not have been  carried over into the post-war  occupation when you did need mass  but it was a remarkable success and i  hope that the less that we don't take a  lesson out of this experience which says  that transformation uh uh was a mistake  or was oversold and that we do in fact  continue down that route for our own  defense policy  thank you richard  thanks kurt it's an honor to be here  discussing this brilliant book by two of  the great military historians of  contemporary america and such an  important conflict and it's an honor to  be speaking in front of people who have  been involved in this  been great comments so far and i just  want to add a couple especially given  that i have to admit i haven't finished  the book yet  when i got it monday afternoon i thought  of calling my buddy kurt and saying kurt  there's only one way guys like you and i  can find the time between now and  thursday to read this book which is to  book a flight to japan right now  and just go and then come back  but but uh but i'm delighted it's  actually this thorough and i will look  forward to finishing it in the days  ahead uh because the military history  piece of it is going to be such a big  contribution but i'm going to as others  have talked more about the specifics on  the policy debates uh just make a couple  of observations one in trying to  understand how we could um  how we could make some of the mistakes  that we made uh and  and richard's done a nice job i think of  of reminding us of the successes and the  mentality and the inherent difficulty of  this but still there were some big  mistakes and i like to think of it as  the the the failure to do a good job  starting on april 9th was really the  flip side of the same logic that led us  to such a brilliant success one didn't  have to follow from the other but  rumsfeld fell in love with his war plan  he fell in love with a plan that said go  for the for the head of the serpent cut  it off and the rest of iraqi society  should be intact and should be something  we can work with because i think it was  the right war plan to try for michael  was i think very michael gordon did a  very nice job of saying there needed to  be a plan b  but it makes sense that in such a  corrupt dictatorship that you would try  to go for the head of the serpent and it  made sense given all the cia contacts  and other special forces uh efforts that  we had made to hope the conventional  army might not fight that hard and in  fact we had some success in keeping the  conventional army largely out of this  fight and largely because they didn't  really support the regime for all the  reasons and with all the all the  implications michael had talked about  there were all these multiple layers of  security the conventional army was many  layers removed from saddam not great  loyalty to him uh not part of his inner  circle not permitted inside of baghdad  there was no particular reason to think  they would want to fight and therefore  every reason to hope that maybe just  maybe they would survive the war and be  willing and able to restore security for  us once it was complete once the  invasion was complete so the the same  logic that i think led to this fairly  smart invasion strategy on balance  despite the problems with the fed yin  that uh bernard and michael have talked  about and which are very real um that  same logic  uh led us to believe that  we had figured out a way to minimize the  resistance from iraqi society writ large  and i think that was true but the  problem is you couldn't count on these  institutions surviving and that's  obvious in hindsight it was also fairly  obvious frankly at the time to me and  most others on the outside that you  needed a plan b  uh but i could still see how working so  hard to come up with  this  this this new invasion plan which flew  in the face of a lot of traditional  centcom thinking uh and frankly rumsfeld  did a good job of challenging some  traditional military brute force  concepts of needing a half million  people a lot of the revisions rumsfeld  made in those six to 12 months leading  up to the war or i guess 15 months  leading up to the war we're fairly smart  the problem is he didn't have time to  get into the details like that if he was  going to ignore phase 4 as a result and  there wasn't enough benefit to doing it  to ignore phase 4 as a result but i  think some of the logic of what he was  doing made sense and was validated by  the success of the invasion the problem  is that in doing that working so hard to  do that he sort of became enraptured by  his own plan and uh  believed that it would work in a way  that it only could be viewed as an  aspiration so cutting off the head of  the serpent nice idea uh drive to the  heart quickly um nice idea maybe the  iraqi security forces like the  conventional army will survive good idea  but the huge mistake was counting on  that happening and not having the plan b  that michael gordon talked about so i  think that that was to me that's how i  understand uh in the book  rightly or wrongly if i've understood it  correctly or not has reaffirmed this  thinking in my own head that uh rumsfeld  worked so hard to come up with a very  good first half of the plan that he  became a little infatuated with his own  clever thinking which was pretty clever  but only went up to  the april 9th 2003. um  that's my take on some of the broad  strategy i want to talk a minute about  the fedine in the south and um  i think this to me is one of the big uh  maybe to me this the single most  important new ground the books breaking  so far in in what i've read of it and  what i've read of the broader iraq  literature because we all think of the  iraq insurgency and i think president  bush rightly describes it as having  three main components uh foreign  jihadists  uh former regime elements like the  fedeen and then the fence sitters the  people who weren't necessarily going to  fight but who chose to over time because  things didn't work very well  because they were sort of mistrustful of  us to begin with they were loyal to  their fellow sunnier brethren almost all  these fence sitters are sunni arab  especially since sauder and his forces  have not been fighting as hard since uh  the summer of o4  and a lot of the a lot of the thinking  so far in the iraq debate i would  summarize as saying if we had only  gotten off to a better start the fence  sitters would have stayed on the other  side of the fence they wouldn't have  become so discouraged they wouldn't have  been as frequently unemployed they  wouldn't have been as angry at us for  doing these uh culturally insensitive  cordon and sweep operations or for uh  efforts to put down the insurgency early  on that led to civilian casualties and  bred more resentment a lot of the  literature has done a nice job pointing  out that that group of fence sitters  might have been much smaller if we had  gotten off to a better start in the  immediate post-invasion period  what i think michael and mick have done  in addition to validating that argument  is to talk about the former regime  elements in much more detail with much  more vividness than we have realized up  until now was possible and actually  point out that that group also might  have been diminished in size a great  deal and to me i've always assumed that  the jihadists sort of came later and the  fence sitters were fence sitters but the  fed yin and the other top regime  loyalists were basically going to be an  inherent part of this insurgency no  matter what and as rich has said you're  going to have a tough mission therefore  to some extent no matter what now i  think i think rich is correct this would  have been tough but what michael and  mick have argued now is we actually  could have gotten a lot of these fedeen  before we even uh  marched on baghdad before we completed  the war or before they had a chance to  flee the south perhaps that's the most  important point and if we had had  follow-on forces of sufficient number uh  and strength in order to follow up and  exploit our opportunities we we wouldn't  have had to make this choice which  turned out to be a hobbsian choice  between continuing the drive to baghdad  and letting these forces stay uh largely  in place except for the ones we took out  in the course of just moving through the  key passes and so uh i think it would  have been tough to foresee this uh it  would have been tough to know that  fedeen were going to fight this way i  think mix essentially acknowledged this  it would have been tough to have good  enough intelligence  to to have this all in the battle plan  in advance but if we had had more  capacity more people on the ground we  could have actually devoted some of the  some of the units the first cav or the  fourth id or others to essentially  taking out the rest of the fedeen to the  extent possible in the south at the same  time as the third infantry division and  the marines kept on moving to baghdad  and perhaps half of that uh who knows  what number but maybe half of that  fedeen element could have been destroyed  in place  if that had been true there was even  more of an opportunity to squelch this  civil uh the civil unrest in this  insurgency in the early months than i  myself had appreciated before what i  think would have been very hard to do  again because the intelligence would  have had to be exquisite or you would  have had to been quick on your feet and  reacting very well but we do have very  good officers in the us military who are  good at thinking on their feet and i  think they had the capability and the  capacity to do some of this without  having to sacrifice the quick drive on  baghdad in order to do so they might  have been somewhat successful so i've  unfortunately become uh even more  critical of the war plan after reading  the parts of the book i've gotten  through because it strikes me that of  those three big groups that make up the  insurgency today  the the core saddam loyalists actually  might have been in a place we could have  more easily targeted them at a moment in  time and we didn't do it uh and and  therefore instead of having to fight two  or three thousand of them to make up a  number we have we have had to fight  seven to ten thousand of them uh and  that number plus all the fence sitters  and the jihadists has allowed this  insurgency to just plateau at a fairly  high level of violent effectiveness uh  one sentence on the next chapter of the  book of kurt i would simply say they  published the book at exactly the right  time because the next year is going to  be more of the same it's just going to  be the iraqis hopefully having more of  the responsibility to win this than we  but our responsibilities are obviously  going to remain quite robust for a long  time so it was a perfect moment to  publish because um i think the the the  next couple of chapters of the next  couple of books written are not going to  look that much different than what we're  reading in the newspapers right now  thanks michael if i could ask some folks  that like to ask questions please  approach the microphones we'll take a  couple and then we'll go to book signing  while you're gathering yourself at the  mic at the microphone let me ask joe  train michael gordon one specific  question i have to say  i went back and read the opening part of  the commanders and it was striking about  how you describe this generation of  military officers that had survived  vietnam that had arrived on the scene  and that put their bold mark on this war  plan and when i read that and i looked  at this book i wondered if you debated  with yourselves about calling this  rather than cobra ii  uh the civilians war in in contrast with  uh uh the commanders or the generals uh  war given the dramatic role frankly and  i would say overriding role that a  number of powerful determined decisive  uh civilian leaders played in the  formulation execution of the military  strategy  let me address that from somewhat  a different angle  if i were about to  write another book  after having done the generals war  and this book and having  um 40 years of service in the marines  i'm not about to write that book because  my wife would be guilty of justified  homicide  but i think i would  look at what has happened to the  american military leadership  elliot cohen of course did a book and  talking about you know civil military  relations with case studies  but i think we have to look at this  the u.s military was deficient the  senior leadership in this war they did  not push back hard enough there is the  culture within the us military to make  your case before your boss  and when he makes a decision you say aye  aye sir and you go out and do the best  you can to carry it out  i think i think that's dodging a  responsibility  and i think that we have to take a  harder look at what we expect of our  military  the military had a pretty good read on  this whole situation from start to  finish  and having gone through two and a half  years of research and study and  discussion  on this  i think the military the solution or the  the result of this war  uh regardless of the legitimacy of going  into it would have been a lot better  but they didn't  they didn't do that there are all sorts  of reasons for that but you have to say  yourself you know they disorder to  defend the constitution they have a  responsibility there is civilian  leadership but something is now lacking  within the u.s military  whether it's the result of the  the defense reorganization act of 1986  the goldwater nichols act which kind of  emasculated the the corporate wisdom of  the joint chiefs of staff whether it's  that or whether it's the business of you  can't get selected for senior rank or  particularly service chief or or  chairman of the joint chiefs of staff  unless you're a team player i don't know  but there's something fundamentally  wrong  and if we don't take a hard look at it  and correct the military culture within  the  within the  the the constitutional obligations that  they have  then i think we're going to have trouble  down the pike again this is a classic  case of the military being ignored and  the military not standing up  sufficiently and the end result is what  we saw we had a pre this precursor of  this  also existed in the vietnamese war  military leadership and its  responsibility  is more than a process and more than an  organization chart  a lot depends upon the blokes that are  in that chart  and i'm not so sure  that we have  a sufficiently  robust system to ensure  that the genius that exists within the  military is allowed to express itself  let's take both questions and then we're  going to unfortunately cut that off so  that we can actually get down to  business at hand which is to sell books  so if i could ask both of you to present  a quick question to anyone on the panel  and then we'll  return to hors d'oeuvres and drinks  thank you uh  national studies um i want to follow up  on this because i think this is really  the central theme here that we need to  work on  if intelligence was the shortcoming  getting us into the war  the civil military relations was the  fundamental shortcoming  as we entered into it and going forward  and i want you to talk about the lessons  learned we really hear about the  concentration of power with the  secretary we hear about the  perhaps the lack of influence by the nsc  um  what do we learn from the civil military  relations  that you can talk about how do we get it  right going forward because really this  is fundamental to our ability to  prosecute military actions in the future  and i don't think it general general i  don't think it's just the military side  that needs questioning so i'm interested  in your comments on that  yes  hi james rosen mclashy newspapers i just  wondered if um if we could look try to  look forward  a little bit  and  if two or three of you could see  could say where do you think we're going  are things as bleak as  they sometimes  appear  and can  president bush's vision  of the future be be salvaged  quick answers from each of you michael  um  well i on the civil military um  front um  obviously i think that um this is i  agree with the comment earlier that the  defense department had an undue degree  of influence and responsibility it was  but it was president bush who decided to  give  responsibility for the post-war  to secretary rumsfeld and actually  signed a directive to that purpose so  this was a conscious decision by the  senior  commander-in-chief  to organize events this way i don't  think  we we should forget that rumsfeld didn't  steal the post-war responsibility was  given that by the president  there were military officials who were  given  a lot of authority in the war and made  their views known but in in the case of  general franks he pretty much accepted  the paradigm that rumsfeld laid out  which is that  um transformation you know that you  could get in and out quickly and it was  on his advice that plans to drop  drawn up to withdraw most of the forces  by september and president bush went on  an aircraft carrier and declared an end  to major combat operations so it wasn't  a voice that was stifled it was um  a voice that was uh kind of um  it was heard but it was within you know  the accepted paradigm i'm not sure i  have  i i think that you know i guess uh two  thoughts i have is that um you know in  his exit interview and we have this in  the book on page 39 because i had to  look it up  is in his exit interview with president  bush  secretary of state colin 